        "DEFINITION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "canonical_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Microbial colonization of the upper urinary tract, including the renal pelvis, collecting diverticula, renal parenchyma, and ureters; because it is not usually limited to the renal pelvis and parenchyma, a more descriptive term is upper urinary tract infection (UTI); this chapter is limited to bacterial pyelonephritis.",
              "pdf_page": 1242,
              "printed_page": 1160,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Infection of any portion of the urinary tract usually requires impairment of normal host defenses against UTI (see Lower Urinary Tract Infection chapters); normal defenses against ascending UTI include mucosal defense barriers, ureteral peristalsis, ureterovesical flap valves, unidirectional flow of urine, and an extensive renal blood supply. Pyelonephritis usually occurs by ascension of microbes causing lower UTI. Hematogenous seeding of the kidneys is an uncommon cause of pyelonephritis. Regardless of the route of infection, an upper UTI is frequently accompanied by lower UTI.",
              "pdf_page": 1242,
              "printed_page": 1160,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Pyelonephritis can develop secondarily to infected nephroliths. Upper UTI with urease-producing bacteria can predispose to struvite nephroliths (see Urolithiasis, Struvite—Dogs).",
              "pdf_page": 1242,
              "printed_page": 1160,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Obstruction of an infected kidney or ureter can rapidly cause septicemia (urosepsis).",
              "pdf_page": 1242,
              "printed_page": 1160,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SYSTEMS AFFECTED": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "canonical_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Renal/urologic.",
              "pdf_page": 1242,
              "printed_page": 1160,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Can cause urosepsis, thus affecting any body system.",
              "pdf_page": 1242,
              "printed_page": 1160,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
