          "canonical_heading": "PATHOLOGIC FINDINGS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Consider pyelonephritis as a rule-out for",
              "pdf_page": 1242,
              "printed_page": 1160,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Kidneys affected by chronic pyelonephritis fever of unknown origin, polyuria/polydipsia, may have areas of infarction and scarring on acute decline in renal function, and/or the capsular surface. The renal pelvis and lumbar/abdominal pain. collecting diverticula may be dilated and",
              "pdf_page": 1242,
              "printed_page": 1160,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    "TREATMENT": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "obtained from the renal pelvis. Pyelocentesis",
          "pdf_page": 1242,
          "printed_page": 1160,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Since many lack specific symptoms can be performed percutaneously using attributable to pyelonephritis, any patient ultrasound guidance or during exploratory with UTI could potentially have pyelo surgery. Urine cultures from the renal pelvis nephritis; the best methods for differentiating usually are similar to cultures from the between upper and lower UTI are bladder unless obstruction is present. ultrasonography or excretory urography.",
          "pdf_page": 1242,
          "printed_page": 1160,
