        }
      ],
      "subsections": {
        "GENERAL": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "GENERAL",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "APPROPRIATE HEALTH CARE": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "APPROPRIATE HEALTH CARE",
          "canonical_heading": "APPROPRIATE HEALTH CARE",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Outpatient treatment with oral antibiotics if the animal is clinically well. Hospitalized care if patient is dehydrated, has decreased renal function or if sepsis is suspected.",
              "pdf_page": 1243,
              "printed_page": 1161,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "NURSING CARE": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "NURSING CARE",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "ACTIVITY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "ACTIVITY",
          "canonical_heading": "ACTIVITY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Unlimited",
              "pdf_page": 1243,
              "printed_page": 1161,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "DIET": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIET",
          "canonical_heading": "DIET",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Renal therapeutic diets are recommended for concomitant CKD.",
              "pdf_page": 1243,
              "printed_page": 1161,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "CLIENT EDUCATION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CLIENT EDUCATION",
          "canonical_heading": "CLIENT EDUCATION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Recurrent pyelonephritis may be asympt omatic. Unresolved chronic pyelonephritis may lead to progression of CKD; diagnostic follow-up is important to document resolution or progression of pyelonephritis.",
              "pdf_page": 1243,
              "printed_page": 1161,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "In patients with infected nephroliths, resolution of pyelonephritis is unlikely unless the nephroliths are removed.",
              "pdf_page": 1243,
              "printed_page": 1161,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SURGICAL CONSIDERATIONS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SURGICAL CONSIDERATIONS",
          "canonical_heading": "SURGICAL CONSIDERATIONS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Complete obstruction of the upper urinary tract of a patient with pyelonephritis may rapidly progress to septicemia and therefore should be regarded as a medical emergency. The cause of the obstruction should be corrected by endoscopic or surgical ureteral stent placement.",
              "pdf_page": 1243,
              "printed_page": 1161,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Infected nephroliths—remove surgically, medically dissolve (struvite), or fragment by extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy; use periprocedural antibiotics to reduce the risk of urosepsis when manipulating infected nephroliths.",
              "pdf_page": 1243,
              "printed_page": 1161,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Unilateral nephrectomy is usually not effective for elimination of suspected unilateral pyelonephritis.",
              "pdf_page": 1243,
              "printed_page": 1161,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "MEDICAL": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "MEDICAL",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "SURGERY": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "SURGERY",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "MEDICAL THERAPY": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "MEDICAL THERAPY",
          "content_blocks": []
